# Local Pi runtime state
.atl/
.venv/
.domain-adaptation-cache/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Created by proposal-implementation: the target's own environment
__pycache__/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# The benchmark's own output travels with it: the harness, the notebooks, the record,
# the results and the figures. The weights do not, and that is a reversal of an
# earlier decision made with a number nobody had measured.
#
# Keeping three checkpoints of every arm — which is what lets the figures be chosen
# after the campaign ranks the arms instead of guessed before it — puts roughly
# 7.7 GB of binaries into the history at full scale. History is the one thing that
# cannot be made smaller later without rewriting it, and a repository nobody can
# clone records nothing.
#
# What stays is everything needed to read the run and everything needed to repeat
# it: the manifests, which record exactly which images formed every bag, beside the
# results, the figures and the record. What is lost is analysing the latent space
# without training first — a real cost, stated rather than discovered.
MIL-CREDA/Models/Benchmark/*.pt
#
# What stays out is the dataset cache, and only that. It is MNIST, USPS and SVHN
# exactly as torchvision downloads them: somebody else's data, 243 MB of it, that a
# single line fetches again. Carrying a copy in this repository would be
# redistributing it, not recording anything about this work.
.benchmark-data/

# tools/bridge.py's own output: a merge of shards reshaped for the report
# notebook to read, reproducible from the shards on disk and never a
# campaign's record — see tools/bridge.py's module docstring.
.scratch/

# remote-execution's local bookkeeping for this repository — created afresh
# by scripts/ledger.py::append() and scripts/remote_cli.py::cmd_fetch() the
# first time either writes here. Two separate decisions, not one:
#
# quarantine/ holds fetched artifacts a fetch judged not current. It is
# machine-regenerable (a re-fetch of the same submission id reproduces it
# byte-for-byte) and large — tens of MB per rehearsal alone. Nothing here
# earns a place in history.
#
# The ledger files themselves (smoke.jsonl and any sibling *.jsonl this
# command writes) are a separate call: they are genuine provenance about
# what ran where, not disposable output. They stay out anyway, for a
# different reason — they are per-machine local execution state keyed to
# whichever account credentials that machine used at rehearsal time, an
# append-only log that would generate merge noise on every local run, and
# not a fact anyone reproducing the benchmark needs: the campaign's own
# record is what lands under Results/, not this queue's bookkeeping.
.remote-execution/
